# --- run every one of them before the functional test below, same rule as
# the four above: a binutils that cannot say what it is is not worth using. ---
./as.exe --version
./ld.exe --version
./dlltool.exe --version

# --- the real point of building as/ld/dlltool: assemble and link a program
# from scratch, entirely through this package's own output, bypassing tcc's
# linker (and its pe_load_def extension) for this one test -- see hello.s's
# own header and default.nix's "ld against ntlibc". ---

# dlltool synthesizes a real import library from ntlibc's .def -- the
# standard-GNU-toolchain answer default.nix's "Import libraries" note
# describes, now actually run. dlltool spawns a real assembler as a child
# process for the small stub objects an import library needs (deduce_name
# in dlltool.c, checked directly) -- it is not the "ar/ranlib/nm/objcopy
# spawn no children" case default.nix's "pex-win32.c vs pex-unix.c" section
# describes, so pex-unix.c's fork()+exec() path (already built in, for
# that reason) is genuinely exercised here for the first time in this
# package. --as names this package's own as.exe explicitly -- there is no
# "as" on PATH to fall back on inside this build.
./dlltool.exe --as=./as.exe -d ${ntlibc}/lib/ntdll.def -l libntdll.a

# as assembles hello.s (freestanding -- see its own header for why it does
# not link against ntlibc's crt1.o/libc.a) into a real i386 PE/COFF object.
./as.exe -o hello.o hello.s

# ld links it against nothing but the dlltool-built libntdll.a -- proving
# ld can consume a synthesized import library the way gcc's own final
# links eventually will, independent of the separate ELF/COFF finding
# above. -L ld/ldscripts: ld.exe is running from
# ${NIX_BUILD_TOP}/binutils-${version} here, not from ${out}/bin where the
# install step below puts a real "ldscripts" next to it for
# find_scripts_dir's own executable-adjacent convention to pick up -- so
# the default script (i386pe.x, read from disk, not compiled in) is named
# explicitly via -L for this in-tree test.
./ld.exe -e _start -o hello.exe hello.o -L . -L ld/ldscripts -lntdll

# And run it: a real PE32 executable, produced by this package's own as+ld,
# resolving its one ntdll import through dlltool's libntdll.a rather than
# tcc's pe_load_def extension, executed here the same way ar.exe's own
# --version above already proved this build environment can run a
# freshly-built PE32 binary. Calls NtTerminateProcess(-1, 0) directly and
# exits 0 (kaem --strict fails the whole build on a nonzero exit, so a
# silent pass here is a real one).
./hello.exe

# --- run every one of them before installing anything: a binutils that
# cannot say what it is is not a binutils worth handing to the next
# stage. ---
./ar.exe --version
./ranlib.exe --version
./nm.exe --version
./objcopy.exe --version

# And a real round trip, ar and ranlib together, over an object this same
# build already produced: archive it, index it, list it back, copy it
# through objcopy, and read its symbols with nm -- the four programs
# actually agreeing on one file, not four version strings in isolation.
./ar.exe rc selftest.a bu_bucomm.o
./ranlib.exe selftest.a
./ar.exe t selftest.a
./objcopy.exe bu_bucomm.o selftest-copy.o
./nm.exe selftest-copy.o

${bin_mkdir} -p ${out}/bin
${bin_cp} ar.exe ${out}/bin/ar.exe
${bin_cp} ranlib.exe ${out}/bin/ranlib.exe
${bin_cp} nm.exe ${out}/bin/nm.exe
${bin_cp} objcopy.exe ${out}/bin/objcopy.exe
${bin_cp} as.exe ${out}/bin/as.exe
${bin_cp} ld.exe ${out}/bin/ld.exe
${bin_cp} dlltool.exe ${out}/bin/dlltool.exe

# ld's own runtime script search (ldfile.c's find_scripts_dir, see
# ld_ldfile.o's compile above) checks for an "ldscripts" directory next to
# its own binary before it ever consults SCRIPTDIR -- so this is what a
# caller invoking ${out}/bin/ld.exe with no -L of its own actually gets.
${bin_mkdir} -p ${out}/bin/ldscripts
${bin_cp} ld/ldscripts/i386pe.x ${out}/bin/ldscripts/i386pe.x
${bin_cp} ld/ldscripts/i386pe.xa ${out}/bin/ldscripts/i386pe.xa
${bin_cp} ld/ldscripts/i386pe.xbn ${out}/bin/ldscripts/i386pe.xbn
${bin_cp} ld/ldscripts/i386pe.xe ${out}/bin/ldscripts/i386pe.xe
${bin_cp} ld/ldscripts/i386pe.xer ${out}/bin/ldscripts/i386pe.xer
${bin_cp} ld/ldscripts/i386pe.xn ${out}/bin/ldscripts/i386pe.xn
${bin_cp} ld/ldscripts/i386pe.xr ${out}/bin/ldscripts/i386pe.xr
${bin_cp} ld/ldscripts/i386pe.xu ${out}/bin/ldscripts/i386pe.xu
